WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.460
Hey, this may or may not surprise some of you.&nbsp;
Yet another Republican has been found guilty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.380
voter fraud. And, you know, I saw the headline,&nbsp;
Former New York election official pleads Guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.060
to Ballot Fraud. I knew it was going to be&nbsp;
a Republican, but I figured I should look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.440
the article just to make sure. And of course, it&nbsp;
was Jason Show Field, a Republican who recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.420
resigned from the Rensselaer County Board of&nbsp;
Elections in New York, admitted he applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:29.700
for absentee ballots using other people's&nbsp;
per other voter's personal information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.760
He pleaded guilty yesterday to applying for&nbsp;
absentee ballots in the names of others. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.700
from Troy, New York. He resigned late last month&nbsp;
and he admits back in 2021, he unlawfully used the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.700
names and birthdates of voters in connection&nbsp;
with 12 absentee ballot applications that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.680
electronically submitted to the New York State&nbsp;
voter absentee ballot application request,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:58.560
Portal portal. He's 43 years old. He admits&nbsp;
he falsely certified that he was those 12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:04.920
different people. He was originally arraigned in&nbsp;
September. His attorney said Scofield maintained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.020
his innocence in the 12 count indictment. He's&nbsp;
scheduled to be sentenced May 12th. He faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:16.020
up to five years in prison, a fine of a quarter&nbsp;
million dollars and supervised release of up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:23.040
three years for each count. The really important&nbsp;
take away here continues to be individual voter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.980
fraud of this kind is very, very rare in the&nbsp;
United States. There's a study by the Brennan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:39.960
Center for Justice. Voter fraud occurs at a rate&nbsp;
of less than 0.0003%. That's like one case of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:46.740
fraud for every 30 million votes that are cast.&nbsp;
And other studies have found the same. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:53.880
when we do find stories of individual voter&nbsp;
fraud, aside from being really rare, they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:00.360
mostly Republicans. And it's a really terrible way&nbsp;
to try to win elections. And the reason is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:06.720
the scale is so small and the risk so big. Voter&nbsp;
fraud of this kind often involves one or a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.600
people trying to sway the outcome of an election,&nbsp;
at least in theory. And even if they get away with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.280
their small scale voter fraud, the impact on the&nbsp;
overall results would rarely be significant. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.560
if it's a really, really, really tiny election,&nbsp;
you know, a town of 800 people in which only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.820
fraction are adults and only a fraction vote, and&nbsp;
it could come down to five votes. And you do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:32.040
with 12 ballots, maybe you can swing an election.&nbsp;
Insanely unlikely, and the risk is too high.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.260
They love to say we have no way of catching these&nbsp;
people. We seem to catch plenty of them. And they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.000
almost exclusively are Republicans who are either&nbsp;
trying to test the system or are saying it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.260
actually Democrats who are doing it. So another&nbsp;
case, relatively rare, but it just so happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.600
that it's yet another Republican sentencing in&nbsp;
May. We'll see if they throw the book at him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.680
which would mean five years. And that would&nbsp;
be pretty interesting to see, I must say.

